  "response": "Proximate cause limits a defendant's liability to harms within the scope of the risk created by the conduct. Although an intervening cause is an event that occurs after the defendant's negligent act and contributes to the plaintiff's injury, foreseeability is the principal test for determining whether an intervening cause is superseding. Any intervening act by a third party automatically severs the chain of proximate causation. A rule of automatic severance and a test that turns on the foreseeability of the intervening event cannot both govern the superseding-cause inquiry, for severance cannot at once be mechanical and contingent on what was foreseeable. The appellate court ruled that an intervening act severs a defendant's liability only when that act is unforeseeable, a holding that shows foreseeability to be the operative measure. Negligent medical treatment of an injury the defendant caused is treated as a foreseeable consequence of the original negligence, which further confirms that foreseeability is what decides whether a later event supersedes.\n\nWhere the parties' narratives diverge, the court's first task is to identify the candidate explanations of the physical sequence that the record will support. In the worksite collapse litigation, at least one of two conditions obtained, and perhaps both: the scaffolding at the worksite was improperly secured by the contractor, or the wind speeds during the storm exceeded the scaffolding's design limits. Each possibility carries a different legal consequence, since one locates the hazard in the defendant's own workmanship and the other in a force arriving from outside the enterprise.\n\nThe allocation of proof shapes how such a stalemate is broken. The plaintiff must prove causation by a preponderance of the evidence. The defendant bears the burden of disproving causation once negligence has been established. A plaintiff's obligation to carry causation by the greater weight of the evidence and a defendant's obligation to negate causation after negligence is shown cannot both attach to the same element of the claim; whichever party must persuade on causation, the other cannot simultaneously bear that risk of nonpersuasion. Put otherwise, the court determined that the plaintiff retained the burden of proving causation throughout the trial.\n\nCausation is ordinarily a question of fact reserved for the jury, and that assignment of the issue to the factfinder produces the denial of summary judgment on causation wherever material facts about the sequence of events remain genuinely disputed. After summary judgment has been denied on that basis, trial courts resolve irreconcilable factual accounts by assessing the credibility of the competing witnesses. The practical consequence is that conflicting narratives of how an injury unfolded are rarely disposed of as a matter of law; they are settled by the factfinder's judgment about which version of events the testimony will bear.",
